/**
 * Stripe LOCAL payment methods (PayNow, GrabPay, UPI, Alipay, WeChat Pay) for
 * wallet top-ups. PURE — no db, no settings, no Stripe client — so the rules are
 * unit-testable and safe to import from client components. The DB-backed half
 * (which methods are switched on right now) is lib/local-payments.ts.
 *
 * WHY WALLET TOP-UPS ONLY. Every local method here is a one-time, customer-present
 * payment: none can be saved and charged off-session the way a card can, and the
 * platform's renewals draw from a saved card or the wallet. So a local method is
 * offered where money lands in the WALLET (Add funds), and the wallet then pays
 * the order or the renewal. They are deliberately NOT offered as a way to pay an
 * order directly, which would break auto-renewal and the purchase-leg fulfilment
 * (lib/stripe.ts `isPurchaseLegSession`) that assumes a card/wallet funding leg.
 *
 * WHICH METHODS ARE REAL FOR US. A method is only available to a merchant whose
 * Stripe account is registered in a supporting country. The source for the table
 * below is Stripe's payment-method support table as read into the growth backlog
 * on 2026-10-01 (mkt-016): PayNow is Singapore merchants only; GrabPay is
 * Malaysia/Singapore; UPI includes Canada and Singapore; Alipay and WeChat Pay
 * are offered to Canadian accounts. Our Stripe entity's country is NOT verified
 * in the repo (it is read from the `stripe_account_country` setting, blank by
 * default), so with no country set NO local method is offered. Not offered:
 * GCash and QRIS (no native Stripe support for an SG account, no local PSP is
 * integrated) and FPX/PromptPay (we do not hold a MY/TH entity).
 *
 * CHARGE CURRENCY. PayNow and GrabPay can only be charged in SGD, UPI in INR.
 * Nothing in the repo supplies a trustworthy FX rate (lib/currency.ts's table is
 * documented as a coarse display snapshot), so a non-USD method is unavailable
 * until an admin enters a rate for that currency (`local_pay_fx_rates`). The
 * customer is charged `usd x rate x (1 + buffer)` rounded UP; the wallet is
 * credited exactly the USD amount they asked for. Alipay and WeChat Pay are
 * requested in USD (both support it per Stripe's currency table) so they need no
 * rate; if Stripe refuses that for the account, session creation fails cleanly
 * and the customer sees the error — nothing is charged.
 */

